EXT. GLASS ORCHARD - DUSK

Trees of blown glass. Fruit that hums. Wind makes them chime.

BRIAR, 40s, a pruner in a moss-colored coat, works a ladder. Snip. Snip. She doesn't look down.

At the base, TAMSIN, 20s, holds the ladder. Her knuckles are white.

                    TAMSIN
You said you'd stop at dusk.

                    BRIAR
It's not dusk.

Tamsin looks west. The sun is a thumbnail above the hills.

                    TAMSIN
It's nearly.

Snip. A glass pear drops. Tamsin catches it. It sings in her palm, one thin note.

                    BRIAR
Put it in the basket.

She doesn't. She holds it. Closes her fingers around it.

A shadow moves between the trees. Slow. Wrong-shaped.

KESTREL VANE, 60s, a crown of brass antlers, steps into the lane. Bare feet. No sound on the gravel.

Briar stops cutting. The chimes die.

                    KESTREL
Evening, sisters.

                    BRIAR
Not your orchard anymore.

                    KESTREL
Not yours either.

Tamsin's fist tightens. The pear cracks. A hairline of light runs through it.

                    TAMSIN
Briar.

                    BRIAR
Hold the ladder.

The pear shatters. Every tree in the orchard rings at once, a scream of glass.
